  "metadata": {
    "title": "Show the Number: A Verification-First Epistemology for Trustworthy AI, Systems, and Evidence-Based Forecasts",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22850266",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-19",
    "description": "<p>Autonomous AI agents, open-weight model artifacts, and high-stakes forecasts now circulate through supply<br>chains whose claims cannot be checked by inspection. This book develops, applies, and audits a single<br>answer: a verification-first epistemology in which every consequential claim carries a number, every number<br>a threshold, and every threshold an executable falsifier that runs in continuous integration &mdash; the discipline of<br>saying not &ldquo;yes&rdquo; or &ldquo;no&rdquo; but &ldquo;show the number.&rdquo; The volume integrates the complete corpus of a two-year<br>research program into one architecture of evidence: a fail-closed Verifiable Trust Kernel (containment,<br>cryptographic GGUF model attestation, an append-only causal evidence graph anchored in transparency<br>logs, and quorum-governed constitutions); an Orchestration Layer binding identity, provenance, gates, and<br>refusals; counterfactual replay of documented incidents (Bybit, StablR, SUPERFORTUNE); regulatory<br>mapping from the EU AI Act to DORA and ISO/IEC 42001; a 19-conjecture falsification program for the<br>2026 global space sector (6 Measured, 1 Reduced, 10 Unproven, 2 Falsified); applied governance of a<br>market-research ecosystem (19 improvements, 34 verified sources); a seven-conjecture advanced R&amp;D<br>dossier; and companion audits of model artifacts, content-addressed storage, autonomous trading agents,<br>constitutive AGI definitions, sovereign hardware, and the program's own internal references. The<br>consolidated ledger &mdash; 26 registered conjectures, three falsified by recorded events, five documented<br>self-corrections &mdash; is the argument: trustworthy systems are not promised; they are computed, shown, and<br>kept falsifiable.</p>",
